1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,447
Google asked me this exact Kafka question in the second round of my system design interview.

2
00:00:06,397 --> 00:00:09,568
How does Kafka achieve ordering guarantees?

3
00:00:09,568 --> 00:00:13,738
Kafka only guarantees ordering within a single partition.

4
00:00:13,738 --> 00:00:17,727
Messages across partitions have no ordering guarantee.

5
00:00:17,727 --> 00:00:23,193
This is the number one mistake candidates make in system design interviews.

6
00:00:23,193 --> 00:00:28,579
The interviewer wants to hear: use a single partition key for related events.

7
00:00:28,579 --> 00:00:38,034
Here is the follow-up that trips people: if you add more partitions for throughput, you lose ordering for existing keys because of partition reassignment.

